      <p>Google Ads works for law firms when each practice area is treated as its own market. A personal injury claim, a divorce, a criminal charge and a will are different searches from different people, with different urgency and value. Build campaigns by practice area, answer enquiries quickly, and judge every campaign on signed cases, not calls.</p>

      <h2>Every practice area, A to Z</h2>
      <p>I run Google Ads for law firms across consumer and business law, including:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bankruptcy and debt relief</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Business and commercial law</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Criminal defence and DUI</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Employment law</strong>, for employees and employers</li>
        <li><strong>Estate planning, wills and probate</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Family law and divorce</strong>, including custody and child support</li>
        <li><strong>Immigration law</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Intellectual property</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Landlord and tenant law</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Medical negligence and malpractice</strong></li>
        <li><strong><a href="/google-ads-for-personal-injury-lawyers/">Personal injury</a></strong>, including road traffic and workplace accidents</li>
        <li><strong>Real estate and conveyancing</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Tax law</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Workers' compensation</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>If your practice area is not listed, the same approach applies. Personal injury has its own page because it is the most competitive and expensive area to advertise in.</p>

      <h2>One campaign per practice area</h2>
      <p>Mixing practice areas in one campaign lets the cheapest searches take the budget. A firm that wants more personal injury cases ends up paying mostly for family law enquiries, or the other way round. Separate campaigns let each area have its own budget, ads, landing pages and target cost per case.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Urgent areas</strong> such as criminal defence and injury: call-led, with ads only when someone can answer</li>
        <li><strong>Considered areas</strong> such as estate planning and business law: consultation-led, with more information on the page</li>
        <li><strong>High-value areas</strong>: separate bidding and budgets, so they are never starved by cheaper searches</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Intake decides the result</h2>
      <p>The best campaign fails if calls go to voicemail or forms wait until tomorrow. Legal clients often contact several firms and choose the one that responds first. Before increasing spend, check how fast enquiries are answered, who answers them, and whether every enquiry is logged with its source.</p>

      <h2>Stay within the rules</h2>
      <p>Legal advertising is regulated twice: by Google's policies and by your bar or regulator. Avoid guarantees of outcomes, use testimonials and case results only where your rules allow them, and check that claims such as "specialist" are permitted where you practise. Some legal-adjacent services are restricted by Google, so check before you advertise them.</p>

      <h2>Measure signed cases</h2>
      <p>A call is not a client. Some callers need a practice area you do not cover, some are outside your jurisdiction, and some are other firms or marketers. Track qualified enquiries and signed cases by practice area, and send signed cases back to Google Ads so bidding learns what a good enquiry looks like. <a href="/learn/google-ads-enhanced-conversions-for-leads-guide/">Enhanced conversions for leads</a> explains how.</p>

      <h2>Where law firm budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Searches for free legal advice and legal aid, if you do not offer them</li>
        <li>Law school, paralegal and legal job searches</li>
        <li>Searches for practice areas or jurisdictions you do not cover</li>
        <li>Research searches with no intent to hire, such as definitions and forms</li>
        <li>Calls outside the hours someone can answer</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>If the firm already advertises, the first question is whether the account knows which enquiries became signed cases. A <a href="/google-ads-audit/">$100 account analysis</a> checks tracking, practice-area structure and search terms, and gives the fix for each issue, delivered within 3 days. Or start with a <a href="#enquiry">free audit</a>.</p>
